OT (more about TGTNIN than NH):

There are no grey dragons in the editions of TGTNIN that KK was familiar 
with.  However, there has probably been something similar among the 
game's countless supplements.

The same applies to dragon scales.  They exist if a DM sees fit to 
include them.  I played a GTNIN game where an enterprising player pried 
scales from a dead dragon, and took them to a friendly blacksmith to 
have them worked into a shield.  In this campaign, the DM ruled that the 
resulting armor was incredibly durable (improved AC bonus), and worth a 
tremendous amount of gold to a collector, but had no other magical 
properties.

The metalworking took several weeks of game time, and this was only 
after the players had found a blacksmith with sufficient skill and 
chutzpah to attempt it.

To create a full suit of dragon scale armor, the blacksmith demanded a 
sum of gold so great that even players who slew a dragon could not 
afford it.  Not to mention that it would have taken several months of 
full-time work.

NHINTGTNHIN... Nethack must follow hard and fast rules covering every 
possible eventuality.  In TGTNHIN, such matters are usually left up to 
the DM and players to decide.

But to return to the topic and answer the real question, TGTNHIN does 
not have anything analogous to magic resistance, let alone dragon scale 
armor which confers it, so I don't think KK's experience with that game 
would have helped him.

